        "title": "Organic food: why?",
        "text": "Today, many governments are encouraging organic, or natural, farming methods that avoid the use of pesticides and other artificial products. The aim is to show that they care about the environment and about people's health. But is this the right approach?\n\nA  Europe is now the biggest market for organic food in the world, and the market has grown by 25 per cent a year over the past ten years. So why does organic food appeal to some people? The really important thing is that organic sounds more 'natural'. Eating organic food is a way of showing that you are natural, good and caring, different from the masses who live on junk food. As one journalist puts it: 'It feels closer to the source, the beginning, the start of things.' The real wish is to be somehow close to the soil, to Mother Nature.\n\nB  Unlike conventional farming, the organic approach means farming with natural, rather than man-made, fertilisers and pesticides. Methods such as crop rotation improve the quality of the soil and help organic farmers to make up for the lack of man-made chemicals. As a way of producing food, however, organic farming is inefficient in its use of labour and land; there are strict limits to how much food can be produced. Also, the environmental benefits of not using artificial fertiliser are tiny compared with the amount of carbon dioxide produced by transporting food (a large part of Britain's organic produce is shipped in from other countries and carried from shop to home by car).\n\nC  Organic farming is often said to be safer than conventional farming — for the environment and for consumers. Yet studies of organic farming around the world continue to reject this claim. A large review by the UK Food Standards Agency found that there was no statistically significant difference between organic and conventional crops. Even where the results suggested a difference, the reviewers found no sign that these differences would have any noticeable effect on health.\n\nD  The simple claim that organic food is more nutritious than conventional food was always likely to be misleading. Food is a natural product, and the health value of different foods varies for many reasons, including freshness, the way the food is cooked, the type of soil it is grown in, the amount of sunlight and rain the crops have had, and so on. In the same way, the flavour of a carrot has less to do with whether it was fertilised with manure or with something out of a plastic sack than with the variety of carrot and how long ago it was dug up. The differences caused by these things are likely to be greater than any differences caused by using an organic or non-organic system of production. In fact, even some 'organic' farms are quite different from one another.\n\nE  The idea that organic food is safer than 'normal' food is also contradicted by the fact that many of our most common foods are full of natural toxins. Parsnips cause blisters on the skin of farm workers. Toasting bread produces carcinogens. As one research expert says: 'People think that the more natural something is, the better it is for them. That is simply not the case. In fact, the opposite is true: the closer a plant is to its natural state, the more likely it is to poison you. Naturally, many plants do not want to be eaten, so we have spent 10,000 years developing agriculture and breeding harmful traits out of crops.'\n\nF  Yet educated Europeans are more afraid of eating traces of a few strictly controlled man-made chemicals than of eating the ones that nature created directly. Because they are surrounded by plenty of food, it is not nature they worry about, but technology. Our obsessions with the ethics and safety of what we eat — concerns about antibiotics in animals, additives in food, GM crops and so on — are symptoms of a highly technological society that has little faith in its ability to use this technology wisely. In this context, the less something is touched by human hands, the healthier people assume it must be.\n\nG  In the end, the organic farming movement is an expensive luxury for shoppers in well-groomed Europe. For developing parts of the world, it is irrelevant. To European environmentalists, the fact that organic methods need more labour and land than conventional ones to produce the same yields is a good thing; to a farmer in rural Africa, it is a disaster. There, the land tends to be so poor and crop yields so low that there simply is not enough organic matter to put back into the soil. Perhaps the focus should be on helping these countries to gain access to the most advanced farming methods, rather than going back to basics."
